WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
$573 BILLION BET:
Apple has invested $573 billion into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:13.200
this over the past 10 years, can you guess what it&nbsp;
is? Maybe R&amp;D? Well, Apple spends about $6 to $7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:19.620
billion per quarter on R&amp;D. So, while that adds&nbsp;
up to about $250 billion over a 10 year period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:25.740
that’s less than half of this other investment.&nbsp;
Maybe they spent it on acquisitions? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:31.320
Apple only has 2 acquisitions that even cross the&nbsp;
billion dollar mark: Beats and Intel’s Smartphone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:38.280
Modem Business. So, nowhere near $573 billion.&nbsp;
Maybe they’ve been hoarding a crap ton of cash?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:44.640
Well, Apple has actually be decreasing their&nbsp;
cash reserves consistently from over $100 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:51.060
down to just over $50 billion. Ok then, maybe&nbsp;
it’s the total value of all of Apple’s assets?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:57.360
Well, while Apple’s assets does tower at an&nbsp;
impressive $350 billion, it still falls over $200&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:04.980
billion short of $573 billion. But then, if Apple&nbsp;
didn’t spend this money on R&amp;D, acquisitions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:11.700
cash reserves, or investments, where the hell&nbsp;
did such a ginormous amount of money go? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:18.000
it turns out that Apple’s biggest bet over&nbsp;
the past 10 years is actually stock buybacks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:25.560
That’s right, Apple has spent $573 billion&nbsp;
over the past 10 years on stock buybacks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:32.760
For perspective, that’s enough money to buy a&nbsp;
fortune 10 company cash. In 2nd place, we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:39.540
Microsoft who comes in at a measly $170 billion&nbsp;
over the past 10 years or over $400 billion less.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:46.320
It looks like disparity is only going to grow&nbsp;
larger as Apple is simply doubling down. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.960
nowadays, Apple is spending nearly a $100&nbsp;
billion on stock buybacks every single year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.720
Assuming that this continues to increase, Apple&nbsp;
will spend over a trillion dollars on buybacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:03.420
over the next 10 years. This has sparked rumors&nbsp;
regarding whether Apple is trying to go private.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:09.000
But more importantly, it has raised concerns&nbsp;
about whether this is healthy for the econonmy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.200
Instead of investing this money into&nbsp;
creating new products, creating new jobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:19.860
or even just storing it in cash or bonds where it&nbsp;
can be lent out, Apple has decided to spend half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:26.220
a trillion on inflating their stock price.&nbsp;
So, here’s the story of how a once banned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:32.660
practice became so popular and why companies are&nbsp;
throwing trillions at buybacks every single year.

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:38.580
A BANNED PRACTICE:
The reasoning behind why companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:44.400
love stock buybacks is not rocket science. By&nbsp;
purchasing shares from the market and burning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:50.340
them, companies are not only adding demand to&nbsp;
the market but they’re also reducing supply. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:56.040
thanks to basic supply and demand economics, this&nbsp;
dynamic leads to stock prices going up. Aside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:01.740
from boosting the stock price, buybacks also boost&nbsp;
the benefits of being a shareholder. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:08.400
let’s say that Apple had a total of 100 shares of&nbsp;
stock and that you owned 10 of them. This means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.680
that you own 10% of the company, but what if&nbsp;
Apple came in and eliminated 20 of these shares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:19.680
by buying them back. Well, you still only own 10&nbsp;
shares, but since the total number of shares has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:26.880
decreased to 80, you now own 12.5% of Apple. This&nbsp;
means that you’ll now get 12.5% of the dividends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:33.360
12.5% of the voting power, and so on and so forth.&nbsp;
So, it’s no wonder why companies and shareholders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:40.140
love buybacks, but the impact of buybacks on&nbsp;
the economy is not so bright. This is why stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:46.680
buybacks were banned for over 50 years up until&nbsp;
1981. You see, the idea of companies buying back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:51.660
their own stock is by no means a new one.&nbsp;
It’s only natural that once companies run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:57.240
out of external places to invest, they turn to&nbsp;
investing in themselves. But, ironically, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:02.520
roots of buybacks actually trace back to times of&nbsp;
despair as opposed to times of prosperity to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:07.620
great depression. A lot of activists who are not a&nbsp;
fan of buybacks like to claim that stock buybacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:13.320
are what led to the great depression in the first&nbsp;
place but this is not true. The primary reasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:18.300
for the great depression were simply excessive&nbsp;
speculation paired with excessive levels of debt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:24.840
But while buybacks didn’t cause the depression,&nbsp;
the depression did cause buybacks and buybacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:30.660
only made the depression worse. For obvious&nbsp;
reasons, investors became frantic when the Dow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:36.600
Jones crashed 90% within a matter of 3 years. They&nbsp;
demanded that executives put up strong quarterly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:43.560
reports, increase dividends, and basically defy&nbsp;
the economic situation. Understandably, this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:50.160
simply not possible for most companies given the&nbsp;
economic landscape, but they were able to provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:56.460
investors some solace through buybacks. While&nbsp;
this didn’t change the fact that most investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:01.800
were down a ridiculous amount, it did ensure that&nbsp;
they could maximize the wealth that they did still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:07.740
have. So, buybacks quickly became the go to way&nbsp;
to pander to shareholders during the depths of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:14.220
great depression, but this was terrible for the&nbsp;
economy. To escape the depression, America needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:20.820
corporations to invest in employees, factories,&nbsp;
and R&amp;D. But instead, companies were spending the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:26.220
little money they did have on superficial avenues.&nbsp;
This went on for a couple of years until the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:33.420
Securities Act of 1933 and the Securities Exchange&nbsp;
Act of 1934. This legislation wasn’t targetted at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:39.180
stock buybacks specifically, in fact, it doesn’t&nbsp;
even mention buybacks. Rather, it was designed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:44.220
prevent the depression from happening again by&nbsp;
putting some checks and balances in place. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:48.900
of these checks and balances was that companies&nbsp;
were not allowed to manipulate their own stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:56.040
price. Technically, there was no mention of stock&nbsp;
buybacks, so that was more of a gray area, but no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:02.100
company was brave enough to test the SEC. So, the&nbsp;
practice of stock buybacks simply disppaeared into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:07.920
oblivion. It would’ve been great if it stayed&nbsp;
that way, but that obviously wasn’t the case.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:14.220
BUYBACKS MAKE A COMEBACK:
For the next few decades, no one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:19.920
even mentioned the idea of stock buybacks. The&nbsp;
entire world was in recovery mode both from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:27.240
Great Depression and WW2. America was also super&nbsp;
focused on showing up the USSR with capitalism, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:32.700
allowing corporations to inefficiently spend their&nbsp;
reserves on buybacks was out of the question. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:37.500
as the stock market blew past its pre depression&nbsp;
highs and America landed people on the moon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:43.920
congress became a lot more relaxed. They were no&nbsp;
longer out to prove something, they had already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:51.000
come out on top. So, now they were able to focus&nbsp;
on personal gain, and what’s the easiest way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:55.560
make money as a politician? Well, the answer&nbsp;
is the align yourself with the richest business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.560 --> 00:07:00.960
people and corporations around the world. This&nbsp;
coincided with yet another economic disaster:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:07.260
the inflation crisis of the 1970s. In the&nbsp;
early 1970s, President Nixon would end the gold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:13.560
standard meaning that US dollars were no longer&nbsp;
backed by gold. This led to inflation spiraling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:21.000
out of control, the stock market stagnating, and&nbsp;
interest rates rising to a painful 19%. So, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:26.400
President Ronald Reagan stepped in and suggested&nbsp;
doubling down on capitalism, people were more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.620
receptive. The logic was that capitalism is what&nbsp;
saved America from the Great Depression and made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:37.740
America into a world power. So, if we wanted to&nbsp;
get back on track, we should let capitalism do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:43.500
its magic. With that, President Reagan would&nbsp;
enact a slew of pro capitlist legislation now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:49.920
known as reagaonomics. This included things like&nbsp;
slashing personal income tax from 70% down to 28%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:57.180
reducing corporate tax from 48% down to 34%,&nbsp;
and easing regulation substantially. One of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:04.980
these regulation reducing moves was Rule 10B-18&nbsp;
introduced by the SEC in 1982. As you may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:10.320
already guessed, this rule created a safe harbor&nbsp;
for stock buybackas as long as companies abided by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:16.260
4 stipulations. These stipulations governed when,&nbsp;
where, and how companies were allowed to buyback&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:22.380
stock, but complying with these rules is actually&nbsp;
completely voluntary. As we previously touched on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:28.980
the SEC never actually outright banned stock&nbsp;
buybacks. So, companies could still try to push&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:34.440
the limits and buyback stock without following&nbsp;
these stipulations. But, given that they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:40.080
pretty easy stipulations to follow, no one has&nbsp;
really pushed the boundaries. And with that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:46.080
stock buybacks were officially legal once again,&nbsp;
but the practice didn’t catch on as quickly as you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:53.340
might expect. You see, back in the 1980s, value&nbsp;
investing was way more popular than it is today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:58.440
and one of the key attributes that value&nbsp;
investors were looking for was a strong and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:04.080
increasing dividends. So, whenever companies had&nbsp;
extra cash lying around, they far preferred to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:09.360
just pay out larger dividends than buyback&nbsp;
stock. Not to mention, companies used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:15.420
have much more modest profit margins of 10 to&nbsp;
20%. So, they couldn’t meaningfully buy back a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:20.520
substantial portion of the company anyway. But,&nbsp;
all of this would change with the rise of tech.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:24.600
APPLE BETS BIG:&nbsp;

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:29.580
Over time, the mentality of corporations&nbsp;
and shareholders slowly shifted and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:33.960
is beautifully documented by statements from&nbsp;
the Business Roundtable which is a coalition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:41.580
of top CEOs. In 1981, this was the purpose&nbsp;
of corporations as defined by these CEOs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:44.220
“Corporations have a responsibility, first of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:48.060
to make available to the public quality&nbsp;
goods and services at fair prices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:53.820
thereby earning a profit that attracts investment&nbsp;
to continue and enhance the enterprise, provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.820 --> 00:10:03.180
jobs, and build the economy.” But, by 1997, this&nbsp;
was the purpose of corporations. The principal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:09.660
objective of a business enterprise is to generate&nbsp;
economic returns to its owners. If the CEO and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:14.880
directors are not focused on shareholder value,&nbsp;
it may be less likely the corporation will realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:21.000
that value. This probably sounds familiar as this&nbsp;
is how companies are run today. The sole purpose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:26.760
of most companies and executives is to serve&nbsp;
shareholders as best as possible. So, stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:32.220
buybacks started looking more and more favorable,&nbsp;
but again, no company could casually afford to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:38.400
spend tens if not hundreds of billions on stock&nbsp;
buybacks until Apple or actually more specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:43.560
the iPhone. Apple didn’t instantly turn to&nbsp;
stock buybacks either though. For the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:47.760
5 years after the launch of the iPhone, they had&nbsp;
plenty of expenses regarding scaling production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:54.240
marketing, and R&amp;D. But, by 2013, Apple had&nbsp;
achieved a pretty strong financial position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.240 --> 00:11:00.480
leading to their first stock buybacks. Apple was&nbsp;
by no means shy either. They didn’t start off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:08.160
with a modest $1 or $2 billion buyback. They went&nbsp;
straight for a $10 billion stock buyback in 2012,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:13.860
and less than 12 months later, they would announce&nbsp;
a record breaking stock buyback of $60 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:20.580
And that was it, from then on out, Apple has&nbsp;
been the undisputed king of stock buybacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:26.040
pouring in tens of billions into buybacks every&nbsp;
single year. All of this brings up the question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:32.820
is Apple trying to go private and is that even&nbsp;
possible? Well, with Apple’s market cap ballooning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:39.120
to $2 to $3 trillion, it’s becoming less and less&nbsp;
possible, but in the late 2010s, it was very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:47.040
possible. In fact, in 2017, Apple was on track to&nbsp;
buyback 50% of itself by 2020. So, going private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:53.340
was not out of reach per se but this was never&nbsp;
part of the plan. You see, when Apple buys back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:58.560
shares, they’re not putting these shares in some&nbsp;
sort of company treasury. Rather, they’re burning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:03.960
the shares and removing it from the supply. So,&nbsp;
the shares that Apple is buying back are basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:09.540
being donated to all shareholders in proportion&nbsp;
to their ownership. So, with that being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:12.540
Apple is by no means going private despite buying&nbsp;
up hundreds of billions of dollars worth of stock.

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:14.680
THE TIP OF THE ICEBERG:&nbsp;

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:23.520
People are often critical of stock buybacks, but&nbsp;
the reality is that stock buybacks are just the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:29.280
tip of the iceberg. The bigger underlying trend&nbsp;
is that the entire purpose of corporations is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:34.620
shifting. Originally, the purpose of corporations&nbsp;
was to create value in the market place while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:40.860
generating a respectable profit. But, nowadays,&nbsp;
the sole purpose of corporations is to serve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:46.800
shareholders no matter the cost. Stock buybacks&nbsp;
are just one example of this trend but they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:51.780
by no means the only issue. With that being&nbsp;
said though, it doesn’t seem like this trend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:57.780
will be changing anytime soon because that’s&nbsp;
not in the executives’ best interest. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:02.880
as companies engage in stock buybacks, lobby&nbsp;
for lower taxes, lobby for less regulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:08.400
and maximize profits, shareholders are much&nbsp;
more willing to pay executives more and more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:14.220
Back in 1965, the average CEO was&nbsp;
paid 20 times the average worker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:22.200
Today, the average CEO is paid 399 times&nbsp;
the average worker in the same company. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:27.120
as long as this ratio keeps increasing, you&nbsp;
can bet that Apple and every other company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:33.360
will continue betting big on stock buybacks.&nbsp;
Did you realize how much Apple has spent on&nbsp;&nbsp;

